From cef37f5ceaa11698b6e62f4e3e335f8fff5c2624 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 06:23:08 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] qos: fix the defects found in the end-to-end review

Review of the 449-line Reaper delta in rc/qos.c plus its defaults, GUI and
httpd paths, prompted by a field report of high in-game latency. The
report itself is NOT explained by any of these -- see the note at the end.

CLASSIFICATION / POLICY

* qos_dscp_wmm now defaults OFF. It defaulted to "1", and the code only
  disables on a literal "0", so every factory-reset box running type 11
  stamped Q1 CS6 and Q2 CS5 on all LAN-bound traffic. The shipped preset
  classifies every HTTP/HTTPS flow under 512 KB as Q1, so ordinary web
  browsing was promoted to AC_VO (Voice) on the air, where it preempts
  latency-sensitive traffic sitting in AC_BE and aggregates poorly. The
  same feature already caused one field regression (v1.5.3 demoted Q5 to
  AC_BK: 1.5% loss, 77 ms jitter on a healthy 6 GHz client); the lift half
  was never re-examined. Opt-in now, exactly as qos_ipolicer became after
  its own regression.

* qos_default 3 -> 4. The class template is
    0 Web/VoIP  1 Streaming  2 Gaming  3 Downloads  4 Default
  and the catch-all class is qos_default + 1, so "3" put every unmatched
  flow -- games included -- into Q4 "Downloads", the same queue the
  >512 KB File Transfer preset targets. Unmatched traffic now goes to the
  class actually named Default.

* DSCP->class map un-inverted. It sent the GAMING codepoints (CS4/AF4x)
  to Q2 "Streaming" and the STREAMING codepoints (CS3/AF3x) to Q3
  "Gaming". A game that marked DSCP correctly could never reach the queue
  named for it. CS1 (scavenger) moved from Q5 -- the catch-all -- to Q4
  "Downloads", which is the actual bulk class.

CORRECTNESS

* Committed rates are now budgeted. The shipped template asks for
  80+10+5+3+2 = 100% and a hand-tuned 20x5 also lands on exactly 100%,
  leaving qid 0 (unclassified) and qid 6 (LAN/multicast) with no
  guarantee and the scheduler with no slack. The table is parsed up front
  and anything over QOS_MINRATE_BUDGET_PCT (85) is scaled down
  proportionally, preserving the operator's ratios, with a log line.

* Hardware QoS refuses to arm with no upload bandwidth. Every rate
  element sits behind its own "> 0" guard, so a blank qos_obw programmed
  the classification chain, PI2 on all seven queues and the WRR/L4S queue
  config while emitting no shaper, no port shaper and no policer: the
  full cost of QoS with none of its benefit, silently. Field-observed on a
  tester's box (both bandwidth fields empty, qos_enable=1, qos_type=11).
  Both engines now log why and leave the queues stock, and
  add_iQosRules() skips the classful chain too, so the box behaves
  exactly as QoS-off rather than "reclassified but unshaped".

* calc() genuinely widens now. (unsigned long) is 32-bit on this ARM
  target, so the cast widened nothing and bw*pct wrapped above ~42.9
  Gbit/s. Not reachable at today's rates, but it was doing none of what
  it looked like it was doing.

ROBUSTNESS

* Classful gained the idempotence guard and tmctl error logging that the
  2026-07-29 audit (F2) gave type 10 and never back-ported. start_iQos
  re-runs on every WAN event and both setqcfg and setqdropalg
  destructively rewrite the LIVE queue config with traffic in flight.
  Keyed on a djb2 signature of the config nvram, emitted as a plain
  number so nothing user-controlled reaches the generated shell.

* The apply script's exit status is no longer discarded on either engine.

* qos_cake_rtt gained a default; it was the only reaper QoS key without
  one, so it never appeared in nvram and the knob was undiscoverable.

HARDENING

* httpd now grammar-checks qos_orates and qos_sched on save, the missing
  half of the defence-in-depth the bandwidth fields already had. Not a
  known escape -- rc/qos.c bounds every value before use.

NOT A DEFECT, examined and cleared: Reaper_QoS.asp escapes every
nvram-derived value reaching innerHTML via bwlEsc(); MODES is static
tokens and the remaining sink interpolates formatted numbers.

NOTE ON THE FIELD REPORT: none of the above explains 150 ms on an IDLE
link. Metal readbacks are correct (port 2252800, qid4 2140160), qid4
qsize 2728 caps queueing at ~15 ms, and a PC->router ping is flat at
2-4 ms. The affected client is on MLO across mismatched links (6 GHz
320 MHz + 5 GHz 80 MHz), which remains the open lead. These are defects
found alongside it, not a fix for it.

NOT BUILT. Owes a metal test and a port to the four sibling branches.
---
 release/src/router/httpd/web.c       |  17 +++
 release/src/router/rc/qos.c          | 155 ++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 release/src/router/shared/defaults.c |  16 ++-
 3 files changed, 172 insertions(+), 16 deletions(-)

diff --git a/release/src/router/httpd/web.c b/release/src/router/httpd/web.c
index f559ccbdf9..36191b22eb 100644
--- a/release/src/router/httpd/web.c
+++ b/release/src/router/httpd/web.c
@@ -4897,6 +4897,23 @@ int validate_apply(webs_t wp, json_object *root)
 					dbg("reject out-of-range qos_type=%s\n", value);
 					continue;
 				}
+				/* Same reasoning applied to the class table (2026-08-10 review): these
+				 * drive the per-class shapers and the scheduler mode in the generated
+				 * script. rc/qos.c parses them with sscanf/strsep and bounds every
+				 * value before use, so this is not a known escape - it is the missing
+				 * half of the defence-in-depth the bandwidth fields already had.
+				 *   qos_orates  "min-ceil,min-ceil,..."   digits , -
+				 *   qos_sched   "p" | "w<1-63>" x5        p w digits , */
+				if(!strcmp(name, "qos_orates") && *value &&
+				   strspn(value, "0123456789,-") != strlen(value)){
+					dbg("reject malformed qos_orates=%s\n", value);
+					continue;
+				}
+				if(!strcmp(name, "qos_sched") && *value &&
+				   strspn(value, "pw0123456789,") != strlen(value)){
+					dbg("reject malformed qos_sched=%s\n", value);
+					continue;
+				}
 
 				/* log no prefix nvram */
 				nvram_modify_log(name, nvram_safe_get(name), value, activity_obj);
diff --git a/release/src/router/rc/qos.c b/release/src/router/rc/qos.c
index 6efd425c95..658aab8607 100644
--- a/release/src/router/rc/qos.c
+++ b/release/src/router/rc/qos.c
@@ -371,10 +371,43 @@ end:
 	QOSLOG("is_ip=%d, is_mac=%d, is_range=%d, type=%d, new=%s", is_ip, is_mac, is_range, *type, new);
 }
 
+/* Committed rates are a budget, not a wish list: they may not consume the whole
+ * port. The shipped template asked for 80+10+5+3+2 = 100%% and a hand-tuned
+ * 20x5 also lands on exactly 100%%, which leaves qid 0 (unclassified) and qid 6
+ * (LAN/multicast) with no guarantee at all and the scheduler with no slack.
+ * Anything over this budget is scaled down proportionally, preserving the
+ * operator's intended ratios. */
+#define QOS_MINRATE_BUDGET_PCT	85
+
+/* djb2 over the nvram that determines the emitted script. Used only to decide
+ * whether a re-apply would be a no-op, so collisions cost a redundant rewrite,
+ * never a wrong config. Emitted as a plain number, so nothing user-controlled
+ * reaches the generated shell. */
+static unsigned qos_cfg_sig(const char *wan_ifname)
+{
+	static const char *keys[] = { "qos_obw", "qos_ibw", "qos_orates", "qos_sched",
+				      "qos_l4s", "qos_minrates", "qos_pshaper", "qos_ipolicer" };
+	unsigned h = 5381;
+	const char *s;
+	int k;
+	for (s = wan_ifname; s && *s; s++) h = ((h << 5) + h) ^ (unsigned char)*s;
+	for (k = 0; k < (int)(sizeof(keys)/sizeof(keys[0])); k++) {
+		h = ((h << 5) + h) ^ 0x2c;
+		for (s = nvram_safe_get(keys[k]); s && *s; s++)
+			h = ((h << 5) + h) ^ (unsigned char)*s;
+	}
+	return h;
+}
+
 static unsigned calc(unsigned bw, unsigned pct)
 {
-	unsigned n = ((unsigned long)bw * pct) / 100;
-	return (n < 2) ? 2 : n;
+	/* unsigned long is 32-bit on this ARM target, so the old
+	 * (unsigned long) cast widened nothing and bw*pct wrapped above
+	 * ~42.9 Gbit/s. Not reachable at today's link rates, but the cast was
+	 * doing none of what it looked like it was doing. */
+	unsigned long long n = ((unsigned long long)bw * pct) / 100;
+	if (n > 0xFFFFFFFFULL) n = 0xFFFFFFFFULL;
+	return (n < 2) ? 2 : (unsigned)n;
 }
 
 void del_EbtablesRules(void)
@@ -928,10 +961,19 @@ static int add_qos_rules(char *pcWANIF)
 	 * with the documented HW-offload freeze semantics for type 11. */
 	if (IS_HWQOS_CLASSFUL() && nvram_match("qos_dscp_trust", "1")) {
 		static const struct { const char *dscp; int cls; } dscp_map[] = {
-			{ "EF", 1 }, { "CS7", 1 }, { "CS6", 1 }, { "CS5", 1 },	/* voice / interactive     -> Q1 */
-			{ "CS4", 2 }, { "AF41", 2 }, { "AF42", 2 }, { "AF43", 2 },	/* video conf / gaming     -> Q2 */
-			{ "CS3", 3 }, { "AF31", 3 }, { "AF32", 3 }, { "AF33", 3 },	/* streaming / signaling   -> Q3 */
-			{ "CS1", 5 },						/* scavenger / bulk        -> Q5 */
+			/* Classes are named by the shipped template:
+			 *   Q1 Web/VoIP   Q2 Streaming   Q3 Gaming   Q4 Downloads   Q5 Default
+			 * The map used to send the GAMING codepoints (CS4/AF4x) to Q2
+			 * "Streaming" and the STREAMING codepoints (CS3/AF3x) to Q3
+			 * "Gaming" -- exactly inverted, so a game that marked DSCP
+			 * correctly never reached the queue named for it. CS1 (scavenger)
+			 * also landed in Q5, which is the catch-all default class, giving
+			 * deprioritised bulk the same treatment as unmatched traffic;
+			 * Q4 "Downloads" is the bulk class and is where it belongs. */
+			{ "EF", 1 }, { "CS7", 1 }, { "CS6", 1 }, { "CS5", 1 },	/* voice / interactive   -> Q1 Web/VoIP  */
+			{ "CS3", 2 }, { "AF31", 2 }, { "AF32", 2 }, { "AF33", 2 },	/* streaming / signaling -> Q2 Streaming */
+			{ "CS4", 3 }, { "AF41", 3 }, { "AF42", 3 }, { "AF43", 3 },	/* interactive / gaming  -> Q3 Gaming    */
+			{ "CS1", 4 },						/* scavenger / bulk      -> Q4 Downloads */
 		};
 		int di;
 		for (di = 0; di < (int)(sizeof(dscp_map)/sizeof(dscp_map[0])); di++) {
@@ -2804,6 +2846,15 @@ int add_iQosRules(char *pcWANIF)
 		return -1;
 	}
 
+	/* Classful with no bandwidth never reaches the shaper (start_hwqos_classful
+	 * refuses), so installing its classification chain would leave marking and
+	 * DSCP rewriting active with nothing shaping -- the same all-cost/no-benefit
+	 * state, just in iptables. Tear down and stay out, exactly as IS_HWQOS does. */
+	if (IS_HWQOS_CLASSFUL() && strtoul(nvram_safe_get("qos_obw"), NULL, 10) == 0) {
+		del_iQosRules();
+		return -1;
+	}
+
 	if (IS_TQOS() || IS_HWQOS_CLASSFUL()) {
 		status = add_qos_rules(pcWANIF);
 	}
@@ -3108,6 +3159,16 @@ int start_hwqos(void)
 
 	obw = strtoul(nvram_safe_get("qos_obw"), NULL, 10);
 
+	/* Same contract as the classful engine: with no upload bandwidth there is
+	 * nothing to shape, and arming would only apply PI2 to qid 0 for no
+	 * benefit. Refuse, and say why. */
+	if (obw == 0) {
+		logmessage("qos", "hardware QoS not started: no upload bandwidth set "
+				  "(qos_obw is empty). Enter your measured upload speed to enable shaping.");
+		_dprintf("[%s] refusing to arm: qos_obw empty\n", __FUNCTION__);
+		return -1;
+	}
+
 	/* wan_ifname is single-quoted in the generated root script; a literal quote
 	 * (or any shell-unsafe char) would break out. wanX_ifname is a kernel-derived
 	 * token, but guard defensively: reject anything that isn't a plain interface
@@ -3164,7 +3225,8 @@ int start_hwqos(void)
 	fclose(f);
 	chmod(qosfn, 0755);
 	run_custom_script("qos-start", 120, "init", NULL);
-	eval((char *)qosfn, "start");
+	if (eval((char *)qosfn, "start") != 0)
+		logmessage("qos", "hardware QoS apply script failed - shaping may not be active");
 
 	return 0;
 }
@@ -3198,6 +3260,8 @@ int start_hwqos_classful(void)
 {
 	FILE *f;
 	unsigned int obw, ibw, burst, rate, ceil, cap, minr;
+	unsigned int crate[5], cceil[5], mtot;	/* pre-parsed class table + minrate total */
+	int cvalid[5];
 	int i, qid, minrates, l4s;
 	int sched_w[5];		/* v4: per-class WRR weight, 0 = strict priority */
 	char *buf, *g, *p;
@@ -3209,6 +3273,21 @@ int start_hwqos_classful(void)
 	 * unset or any value other than literal "0" = on, "0" = off. */
 	minrates = !nvram_match("qos_minrates", "0");
 
+	/* REFUSE TO ARM WITH NO UPLOAD BANDWIDTH. Every rate element below is
+	 * behind its own "> 0" guard, so a blank qos_obw used to program the
+	 * classification chain, PI2 on all seven queues and the WRR/L4S queue
+	 * config while emitting NO shaper, NO port shaper and NO policer: the
+	 * full cost of QoS with none of its benefit, and no warning anywhere.
+	 * Field-observed 2026-08-10 on a tester's box (qos_obw and qos_ibw both
+	 * empty, qos_enable=1, qos_type=11). Bail loudly and leave the queues in
+	 * stock configuration so the box behaves exactly as QoS-off. */
+	if (obw == 0) {
+		logmessage("qos", "hardware QoS (classful) not started: no upload bandwidth set "
+				  "(qos_obw is empty). Enter your measured upload speed to enable shaping.");
+		_dprintf("[%s] refusing to arm: qos_obw empty\n", __FUNCTION__);
+		return -1;
+	}
+
 	/* same shell-safety guard as start_hwqos: wan_ifname is single-quoted in
 	 * the generated root script; reject anything that isn't a plain interface
 	 * name and fall back to an empty (inert) WANIF rather than emit it. */
@@ -3224,10 +3303,25 @@ int start_hwqos_classful(void)
 
 	fprintf(f,
 		"#!/bin/sh\n"
-		"WANIF='%s'\n\n"
+		"WANIF='%s'\n"
+		"SIG='%u'\n"
+		"HWCST=/tmp/qos_hwc_state\n\n"
 		"case \"$1\" in\n"
-		"start)\n",
-		wan_ifname
+		"start)\n"
+		/* Idempotence guard. start_iQos re-runs this on every WAN event, and
+		 * both setqcfg and setqdropalg destructively rewrite the LIVE queue
+		 * config with traffic in flight. The type-10 engine got this guard in
+		 * the 2026-07-29 audit (F2); classful never did. Skip when the wanted
+		 * config is already applied AND the TM still answers for a class queue
+		 * (a queue lost to a link/driver reset falls through and is
+		 * reprogrammed). "force" as $2 overrides. */
+		"\tif [ \"$2\" != \"force\" ] && [ \"$(cat $HWCST 2>/dev/null)\" = \"$WANIF:$SIG\" ] \\\n"
+		"\t   && tmctl getqcfg --devtype 0 --if $WANIF --qid 1 >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n"
+		"\t\texit 0\n"
+		"\tfi\n"
+		"\trm -f $HWCST\n",
+		wan_ifname,
+		qos_cfg_sig(wan_ifname)
 	);
 
 	/* v4: per-class scheduler mode from qos_sched - 5 comma tokens by queue
@@ -3256,10 +3350,37 @@ int start_hwqos_classful(void)
 	 * v3: the min half of each "min-ceil" pair (ignored by v2) is now emitted as
 	 * a committed rate (--minrate) - lower classes keep a guaranteed floor while
 	 * the SP scheduler lets higher classes take the rest (CBWFQ model). */
+	/* Parse the whole class table BEFORE emitting anything: the committed rates
+	 * have to be budgeted collectively, which cannot be done one class at a
+	 * time. */
+	for (i = 0; i < 5; ++i) { cvalid[i] = 0; crate[i] = 0; cceil[i] = 0; }
 	g = buf = strdup(nvram_safe_get("qos_orates"));
 	for (i = 0; i < 5; ++i) {
 		if ((!g) || ((p = strsep(&g, ",")) == NULL)) break;
 		if (sscanf(p, "%u-%u", &rate, &ceil) != 2) continue;
+		crate[i] = rate; cceil[i] = ceil; cvalid[i] = 1;
+	}
+	free(buf);
+
+	/* Apply the committed-rate budget. Without it the guarantees can claim the
+	 * entire port, starving qid 0 / qid 6 (which carry no minrate of their own)
+	 * and leaving the scheduler nothing to arbitrate with. */
+	mtot = 0;
+	for (i = 0; i < 5; ++i)
+		if (cvalid[i] && crate[i] >= 1 && crate[i] <= 100) mtot += crate[i];
+	if (minrates && mtot > QOS_MINRATE_BUDGET_PCT) {
+		logmessage("qos", "class guarantees total %u%% of upload; scaling to %u%% so "
+				  "unclassified and LAN/multicast queues keep a share",
+			   mtot, (unsigned)QOS_MINRATE_BUDGET_PCT);
+		for (i = 0; i < 5; ++i)
+			if (cvalid[i] && crate[i] >= 1 && crate[i] <= 100)
+				crate[i] = (crate[i] * QOS_MINRATE_BUDGET_PCT) / mtot;
+	}
+
+	for (i = 0; i < 5; ++i) {
+		if (!cvalid[i]) continue;
+		rate = crate[i];
+		ceil = cceil[i];
 		qid = i + 1;
 		/* v4: setqcfg FIRST - like setqdropalg it rewrites the whole queue
 		 * config (the v1 ordering lesson), so scheduler mode must land before
@@ -3282,12 +3403,12 @@ int start_hwqos_classful(void)
 		fprintf(f, "\ttmctl setqdropalg --devtype 0 --if $WANIF --qid %d --dropalg 4\n", qid);
 		if (cap > 0) {
 			if (minr > 0)
-				fprintf(f, "\ttmctl setqshaper --devtype 0 --if $WANIF --qid %d --minrate %u --shapingrate %u\n", qid, minr, cap);
+				fprintf(f, "\ttmctl setqshaper --devtype 0 --if $WANIF --qid %d --minrate %u --shapingrate %u"
+					   " || logger -t hwqos \"setqshaper FAILED (rc=$?) qid %d\"\n", qid, minr, cap, qid);
 			else
 				fprintf(f, "\ttmctl setqshaper --devtype 0 --if $WANIF --qid %d --shapingrate %u\n", qid, cap);
 		}
 	}
-	free(buf);
 
 	/* qid 0 (unclassified) and qid 6 (LAN/multicast class): PI2 + full OBW cap */
 	fprintf(f, "\ttmctl setqdropalg --devtype 0 --if $WANIF --qid 0 --dropalg 4\n");
@@ -3303,7 +3424,8 @@ int start_hwqos_classful(void)
 	 * exceeding OBW (the ISP buffer stays out of the game even when several
 	 * classes saturate at once). qos_pshaper="0" kills. */
 	if (obw > 0 && !nvram_match("qos_pshaper", "0"))
-		fprintf(f, "\ttmctl setportshaper --devtype 0 --if $WANIF --shapingrate %u\n", obw);
+		fprintf(f, "\ttmctl setportshaper --devtype 0 --if $WANIF --shapingrate %u"
+			   " || logger -t hwqos \"setportshaper FAILED (rc=$?) on $WANIF\"\n", obw);
 
 	/* v5: download cap - WAN ingress RX policer (kbps, same units as the egress
 	 * shapers; backed by an rdpa policer object, so the flow accelerator stays
@@ -3329,7 +3451,11 @@ int start_hwqos_classful(void)
 		fprintf(f, "\ttmctl setportrxrate --devtype 0 --if $WANIF --shapingrate %u --burstsize %u\n", prate, burst);
 	}
 
+	/* Only record applied-state after the whole start branch has run, so a
+	 * partial failure retries on the next event instead of being cached. */
+	fprintf(f, "\techo \"$WANIF:$SIG\" > $HWCST\n");
 	fprintf(f, "\t;;\nstop)\n");
+	fprintf(f, "\trm -f $HWCST\n");
 	fprintf(f, "\ttmctl setportshaper --devtype 0 --if $WANIF --shapingrate 0\n");
 	/* readback guard: clearing a policer that was never set logs a one-time
 	 * kernel ERR (_drv_policer_clear_profile, metal-observed on first apply
@@ -3355,7 +3481,8 @@ int start_hwqos_classful(void)
 	fclose(f);
 	chmod(qosfn, 0755);
 	run_custom_script("qos-start", 120, "init", NULL);
-	eval((char *)qosfn, "start");
+	if (eval((char *)qosfn, "start") != 0)
+		logmessage("qos", "hardware QoS (classful) apply script failed - shaping may not be active");
 
 	return 0;
 }
diff --git a/release/src/router/shared/defaults.c b/release/src/router/shared/defaults.c
index 98e416cde0..0c73d44319 100644
--- a/release/src/router/shared/defaults.c
+++ b/release/src/router/shared/defaults.c
@@ -3080,13 +3080,19 @@ struct nvram_tuple router_defaults[] = {
 	{ "qos_dscp_trust", "0", CKN_STR1, CKN_TYPE_DEFAULT, CKN_ACC_LEVEL_DEFAULT, CKN_ENC_DEFAULT, 0 },	/* v3 DSCP->class mapping ahead of the default class */
 	{ "qos_sched", "p,p,p,p,p", CKN_STR64, CKN_TYPE_DEFAULT, CKN_ACC_LEVEL_DEFAULT, CKN_ENC_DEFAULT, 0 },	/* v4 per-class scheduler: p = strict priority, w<1-63> = WRR weight */
 	{ "qos_l4s", "0", CKN_STR1, CKN_TYPE_DEFAULT, CKN_ACC_LEVEL_DEFAULT, CKN_ENC_DEFAULT, 0 },	/* v4 EXPERIMENTAL: flag class queues L4S (DualPI2 ECT(1) marking) */
+	{ "qos_cake_rtt", "50", CKN_STR8, CKN_TYPE_DEFAULT, CKN_ACC_LEVEL_DEFAULT, CKN_ENC_DEFAULT, 0 },	/* cake AQM RTT target (ms); was the only reaper QoS key with no default, so it never appeared in nvram */
 	/* reaper QoS v5 (download side): inert until qos_type 11 is active. The RX
 	 * policer defaults OFF ("0"); set "1" to opt in. A policer has no queue, so it
 	 * drops (not delays) excess and clips loss-sensitive real-time UDP (metal
 	 * 2026-07-20: Teams-call packet loss) - so it is opt-in, not on by default.
 	 * When opted in, qos_ibw > 0 arms it. qos_dscp_wmm (WMM-lift half) stays ON. */
 	{ "qos_ipolicer", "0", CKN_STR1, CKN_TYPE_DEFAULT, CKN_ACC_LEVEL_DEFAULT, CKN_ENC_DEFAULT, 0 },	/* v5 download cap: WAN ingress RX policer at qos_ibw; default OFF (opt-in) */
-	{ "qos_dscp_wmm", "1", CKN_STR1, CKN_TYPE_DEFAULT, CKN_ACC_LEVEL_DEFAULT, CKN_ENC_DEFAULT, 0 },	/* v5 downstream class->DSCP stamp (WMM AC lift on wifi egress) */
+	{ "qos_dscp_wmm", "0", CKN_STR1, CKN_TYPE_DEFAULT, CKN_ACC_LEVEL_DEFAULT, CKN_ENC_DEFAULT, 0 },	/* v5 downstream class->DSCP stamp (WMM AC lift on wifi egress) */
+	/* v5 downstream DSCP stamp: OPT-IN as of 2026-08-10. Defaulting it on put
+	 * every HTTP/HTTPS flow under 512 KB -- which the shipped preset classifies
+	 * as Q1 -- into AC_VO (Voice) on the air, where it preempts latency-sensitive
+	 * traffic sitting in AC_BE and aggregates poorly. Same opt-in treatment
+	 * qos_ipolicer got after its own field regression. */
 
 	/* reaper v1.3.0 traffic collector (rtrafd) */
